NAME
       sort - sort lines of text

SYNOPSIS
       sort [OPTION]... [FILE]...

DESCRIPTION
       sort reads the lines of the files (or standard input when no file is
       given, or for -) and prints them in order. The files themselves are not
       changed: redirect the output, or use -o, to keep the result.

       By default lines are compared as text, using the rules of the current
       language (locale). Numbers compared as text come out in an odd order:
       "10" sorts before "9". Use -n to compare numbers by their value.

       A line can be split into fields. Without -t, fields are separated by
       runs of blanks. -k picks the field to sort on.

OPTIONS
       -n, --numeric-sort
              Compare by numeric value.

       -h, --human-numeric-sort
              Compare sizes such as 4K, 12M and 2G (as printed by du -h).

       -r, --reverse
              Reverse the order (largest or last first).

       -u, --unique
              Print only the first of a run of equal lines.

       -f, --ignore-case
              Treat lower-case letters as upper-case.

       -b, --ignore-leading-blanks
              Ignore blanks at the start of lines or fields.

       -M, --month-sort
              Compare month names (JAN < FEB < ... < DEC).

       -V, --version-sort
              Sort version numbers naturally (file2 before file10).

       -g, --general-numeric-sort
              Compare numbers, including ones written like 1.5e3.

       -R, --random-sort
              Shuffle the lines.

       -t, --field-separator=CHAR
              Fields are separated by CHAR, for example -t : for /etc/passwd.

       -k, --key=START[,STOP]
              Sort on fields START to STOP (counting from 1). -k3,3 sorts on
              field 3 only; -k3 sorts on field 3 to the end of the line. A
              letter after the number applies an option to that key only:
              -k3,3n sorts field 3 numerically.

       -o, --output=FILE
              Write the result to FILE. FILE may be one of the input files,
              which a redirection (> FILE) cannot do safely.

       -c, --check
              Only check whether the input is already sorted; report the
              first line out of order.

EXAMPLES
       List the accounts in /etc/passwd ordered by UID, largest first:

           sort -t : -k3,3nr /etc/passwd

       Count how often each login shell is used:

           cut -d : -f 7 /etc/passwd | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr

       Show the five biggest directories under /var:

           du -sh /var/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -5

       Sort a word list in place, removing duplicates:

           sort -u -o /root/words.txt /root/words.txt

NOTES
       uniq only notices duplicate lines that are next to each other, so sort
       first (or use sort -u).

SEE ALSO
       uniq(1), cut(1), tac(1), du(1)
